WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.121
Welcome to Module 9, the last
module of the Attribute-Driven

2
00:00:04.121 --> 00:00:08.025
Migration learning path: deploy
to test, then production.

3
00:00:08.025 --> 00:00:12.200
This module serves every gate,
through Chapter 10's step-to-gate map,

4
00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:16.300
and takes Chapter 10 of Building
the Attribute-Driven Migration.

5
00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:19.951
You'll see what the deployment
runbook adds to the chapters:

6
00:00:19.951 --> 00:00:23.826
one settings file per stage, three
identities, a record of every

7
00:00:23.826 --> 00:00:27.925
change with its rollback, and a go
or no-go record before production.

8
00:00:27.925 --> 00:00:31.195
Then you'll dry-run its five
deploy scripts on your laptop,

9
00:00:31.195 --> 00:00:32.725
against the kits' fixtures.

10
00:00:32.725 --> 00:00:33.925
One thing first.

11
00:00:33.925 --> 00:00:37.353
Chapter 10 is held for
review, and nobody has run its

12
00:00:37.353 --> 00:00:41.025
runbook against a live tenant,
subscription or forest.

13
00:00:41.025 --> 00:00:45.300
The scripts are tested against fixtures
and their dry-run output is real.

14
00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:50.320
Every other result in the chapter is
an expectation, not an observation.

15
00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:56.208
Why does deployment need its own module?

16
00:00:56.208 --> 00:01:01.808
Each chapter's Lab 2 changes the smallest
thing it can, by hand, and backs it out.

17
00:01:01.808 --> 00:01:04.182
That proves one kit in isolation.

18
00:01:04.182 --> 00:01:06.382
A deployment differs in four ways.

19
00:01:06.382 --> 00:01:08.886
It runs all nine kits in a safe order,

20
00:01:08.886 --> 00:01:12.771
and reads every value from
one settings file per stage.

21
00:01:12.771 --> 00:01:14.860
It signs in with three identities,

22
00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:18.145
each with a certificate, and
least privilege per step.

23
00:01:18.145 --> 00:01:20.452
It records every change as it's made,

24
00:01:20.452 --> 00:01:24.033
and writes the rollback from
that record, newest first.

25
00:01:24.033 --> 00:01:28.341
And it doesn't end in the test tenant:
it ends with a go or no-go record

26
00:01:28.341 --> 00:01:32.970
that production's pre-flight check
verifies, then production in waves.

27
00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:37.112
The order is the one this workbook's
implementation guide sets:

28
00:01:37.112 --> 00:01:42.052
prove the attributes first, build groups
and workflows paused or disabled,

29
00:01:42.052 --> 00:01:46.780
target by attribute, cut over by
wave, then keep the attributes true.

30
00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:53.341
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

31
00:01:53.341 --> 00:01:55.705
which takes about fifty
minutes with the lab.

32
00:01:55.705 --> 00:01:59.867
You'll explain the four ways a deployment
differs from a chapter's Lab 2.

33
00:01:59.867 --> 00:02:04.330
You'll describe the settings file, the
placeholders and the three identities.

34
00:02:04.330 --> 00:02:08.905
You'll run the pre-flight check, and say
which checks it runs dry and which live.

35
00:02:08.905 --> 00:02:12.530
You'll record changes and read
the rollback plan they produce.

36
00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:15.988
And you'll explain why a fixture
smoke test can never promote,

37
00:02:15.988 --> 00:02:18.755
and what production's
pre-flight check verifies.

38
00:02:18.755 --> 00:02:22.875
Before you start, do Modules 1
to 8, or at least their Lab 1s:

39
00:02:22.875 --> 00:02:25.830
this module doesn't teach the kits again.

40
00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:29.311
Lab 1 needs Python 3.10
or later with pytest,

41
00:02:29.311 --> 00:02:33.160
and a POSIX shell with
sha two fifty-six sum.

42
00:02:36.910 --> 00:02:41.123
Two ideas make the same commands
run against test and production.

43
00:02:41.123 --> 00:02:47.023
First, one settings file per stage: test
dot json, then production dot json.

44
00:02:47.023 --> 00:02:50.994
It holds every value the commands
need, the cloud, the tenant,

45
00:02:50.994 --> 00:02:54.884
the subscription, the change
record and the kits' settings.

46
00:02:54.884 --> 00:02:57.497
The template's values
are all placeholders.

47
00:02:57.497 --> 00:03:01.724
And there are no secrets, by
construction: the loader refuses a key

48
00:03:01.724 --> 00:03:06.185
or a value that looks like a secret,
and any placeholder left unfilled.

49
00:03:06.185 --> 00:03:07.973
Second, the identities.

50
00:03:07.973 --> 00:03:12.411
Deploy changes Entra, Intune
and Azure in the stage steps.

51
00:03:12.411 --> 00:03:16.647
Reader takes the baselines, the
reconciliation and the smoke tests.

52
00:03:16.647 --> 00:03:21.611
Provisioning sends the HR batches, with
one upload permission and nothing else.

53
00:03:21.611 --> 00:03:23.585
Each signs in with a certificate.

54
00:03:23.585 --> 00:03:27.489
And one named Groups Administrator
signs in for rule validation,

55
00:03:27.489 --> 00:03:30.163
because Microsoft Learn's
page for the group

56
00:03:30.163 --> 00:03:34.573
evaluateDynamicMembership API lists
no application permission for it.

57
00:03:34.573 --> 00:03:38.057
Microsoft Learn's page on signing
in to the Azure CLI with a

58
00:03:38.057 --> 00:03:42.122
service principal recommends
certificate-based authentication.

59
00:03:42.122 --> 00:03:47.025
And splitting the work across three identities
is this workbook's design guidance:

60
00:03:47.025 --> 00:03:52.140
one identity that can do everything is
one credential that can break everything.

61
00:03:55.890 --> 00:03:59.731
The live test stage deploys every
component to the test tenant,

62
00:03:59.731 --> 00:04:02.316
the test subscription and the test forest,

63
00:04:02.316 --> 00:04:06.378
in an order where nothing grants
access before it has been checked.

64
00:04:06.378 --> 00:04:09.214
First, baseline evidence, read only.

65
00:04:09.214 --> 00:04:13.515
Then the HR middleware, as a
dry run and then five records.

66
00:04:13.515 --> 00:04:17.715
Dynamic rules are created paused,
validated, then turned on.

67
00:04:17.715 --> 00:04:22.165
Lifecycle Workflows are created
disabled, with one on-demand run.

68
00:04:22.165 --> 00:04:25.550
Group Policy goes to a
test organizational unit,

69
00:04:25.550 --> 00:04:28.428
with an Intune dry run
and a cut-over drill.

70
00:04:28.428 --> 00:04:32.902
Machine configuration runs in audit
first, then apply and monitor.

71
00:04:32.902 --> 00:04:37.052
Ansible plans its wave and runs
in check mode before it connects.

72
00:04:37.052 --> 00:04:40.139
The drift pipeline's
first run is the baseline.

73
00:04:40.139 --> 00:04:42.493
And the stage ends with the smoke test,

74
00:04:42.493 --> 00:04:46.365
the evidence package, a rollback
drill and the evidence check.

75
00:04:46.365 --> 00:04:48.769
Every change is recorded as it's made:

76
00:04:48.769 --> 00:04:52.180
the rollback is only as
complete as the record.

77
00:04:55.930 --> 00:04:57.055
Then the promotion.

78
00:04:57.055 --> 00:05:00.068
Production's pre-flight
check enforces what it can.

79
00:05:00.068 --> 00:05:04.152
Every requirement row of the test
stage needs passing evidence:

80
00:05:04.152 --> 00:05:05.693
seventeen of seventeen.

81
00:05:05.693 --> 00:05:10.192
The test smoke test must be GO,
from a live run in the test tenant.

82
00:05:10.192 --> 00:05:13.769
Production's settings must
record that exact report's hash,

83
00:05:13.769 --> 00:05:15.229
and the evidence check's.

84
00:05:15.229 --> 00:05:18.627
The CISO and identity
engineering must both approve,

85
00:05:18.627 --> 00:05:21.193
which is this workbook's design guidance.

86
00:05:21.193 --> 00:05:26.679
And test and production must share no
tenant, subscription or deploy identity.

87
00:05:26.679 --> 00:05:29.030
The rest of the criteria are review.

88
00:05:29.030 --> 00:05:33.080
Then production runs the same
commands with production dot json.

89
00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:35.330
The tenant-wide pieces run once.

90
00:05:35.330 --> 00:05:39.304
Then each wave, leaver first,
smoke-tested before the next,

91
00:05:39.304 --> 00:05:42.855
with seven days of chained
drift packages per wave.

92
00:05:42.855 --> 00:05:44.874
And production's rollback rule,

93
00:05:44.874 --> 00:05:49.455
from this workbook's implementation
guide, is re-enable, never re-create.

94
00:05:49.455 --> 00:05:54.360
Roll back the wave, not the tenant:
earlier waves that are drift-clean stay.

95
00:05:58.110 --> 00:05:58.835
Try it.

96
00:05:58.835 --> 00:06:03.009
Lab 1 dry-runs the five deploy
scripts against the kits' fixtures,

97
00:06:03.009 --> 00:06:05.923
in the drift-pipeline
folder of the examples.

98
00:06:05.923 --> 00:06:08.360
Nothing signs in and nothing is called.

99
00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:10.878
Step one starts clean, with no out folder,

100
00:06:10.878 --> 00:06:14.235
because the evidence records
are numbered within it.

101
00:06:14.235 --> 00:06:17.171
Step two runs the test
stage's pre-flight check,

102
00:06:17.171 --> 00:06:20.185
with the production
settings as the other file.

103
00:06:20.185 --> 00:06:24.692
P F oh one to P F ten pass: the
settings schema, no secret in the file,

104
00:06:24.692 --> 00:06:29.978
no placeholder left, the cloud,
certificate paths outside the repository,

105
00:06:29.978 --> 00:06:34.832
all nine kits present, and test and
production on different tenants,

106
00:06:34.832 --> 00:06:37.085
subscriptions and identities.

107
00:06:37.085 --> 00:06:39.647
The test stage needs no promotion record.

108
00:06:39.647 --> 00:06:45.446
The live probes, the Azure CLI's cloud,
tenant, subscription and signed-in identity,

109
00:06:45.446 --> 00:06:49.947
the PowerShell modules and the
certificate files, print as DRY.

110
00:06:49.947 --> 00:06:53.034
The last two lines:
zero failed, a dry run,

111
00:06:53.034 --> 00:06:57.060
and the first evidence
record, preflight pass.

112
00:07:00.810 --> 00:07:05.988
Step three records four changes the way
the stage steps do: the provisioning job,

113
00:07:05.988 --> 00:07:11.010
a dynamic group, a Group Policy link the
stage created, and a policy assignment.

114
00:07:11.010 --> 00:07:13.924
Each add records its change
in the deploy record,

115
00:07:13.924 --> 00:07:16.473
and writes an evidence
record for its step.

116
00:07:16.473 --> 00:07:17.461
Then the plan.

117
00:07:17.461 --> 00:07:22.933
It lists the four undo steps, newest first:
remove the policy assignment, disable

118
00:07:22.933 --> 00:07:28.323
the Group Policy link, remove the dynamic
group, and pause the provisioning job.

119
00:07:28.323 --> 00:07:33.269
Three are cloud steps and one is Active
Directory, so it writes a cloud script,

120
00:07:33.269 --> 00:07:36.959
an A D script and a manual list,
and its own evidence record.

121
00:07:36.959 --> 00:07:39.623
And notice the last line: nothing was run.

122
00:07:39.623 --> 00:07:43.322
You review the scripts, then
run them in a signed-in session.

123
00:07:43.322 --> 00:07:47.480
The rollback is written from the
record, not from memory on the day.

124
00:07:51.230 --> 00:07:53.091
Step four runs the smoke test,

125
00:07:53.091 --> 00:07:57.743
on the fixture validation kit run and
the other kits' fixture provenance.

126
00:07:57.743 --> 00:08:02.279
It reads the gates' checks again after
the change, and answers go or no-go.

127
00:08:02.279 --> 00:08:05.436
It checks that the kit run
matches its own manifest,

128
00:08:05.436 --> 00:08:08.743
and it notes fixture mode, so
the tenant isn't compared.

129
00:08:08.743 --> 00:08:10.004
Then the failures.

130
00:08:10.004 --> 00:08:18.980
Three checks fail: VK-HR-01,
VK-DG-01 and VK-CA-01.

131
00:08:18.980 --> 00:08:22.905
VK-NPI-03 ended in Error.

132
00:08:22.905 --> 00:08:27.918
And the HR dry run has nine rows
quarantined, against a limit of zero.

133
00:08:27.918 --> 00:08:30.805
You'll recognize those
from Modules 1 and 8.

134
00:08:30.805 --> 00:08:33.667
Every change has a
rollback, so that passes.

135
00:08:33.667 --> 00:08:39.092
The answer is NO-GO, five failed, in
fixture mode, and the exit code is two.

136
00:08:39.092 --> 00:08:41.618
The fixtures carry findings on purpose.

137
00:08:41.618 --> 00:08:44.161
The report's hash changes with each run,

138
00:08:44.161 --> 00:08:48.410
because the report records when
it ran, so it isn't shown here.

139
00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:56.035
Step five checks the stage's evidence
against the requirement rows.

140
00:08:56.035 --> 00:08:59.858
Only two of seventeen are
evidenced, both pre-flight rows,

141
00:08:59.858 --> 00:09:02.298
so the check fails with exit code two.

142
00:09:02.298 --> 00:09:03.736
Why are the others missing?

143
00:09:03.736 --> 00:09:05.816
A rollback add entry is recorded:

144
00:09:05.816 --> 00:09:09.748
it proves the change was written
down, not that the step worked.

145
00:09:09.748 --> 00:09:12.584
And the smoke record is
a failing fixture run.

146
00:09:12.584 --> 00:09:17.260
Then sha two fifty-six sum confirms
every evidence record reads OK.

147
00:09:17.260 --> 00:09:19.947
Step six asks production's
pre-flight check

148
00:09:19.947 --> 00:09:22.410
to promote on that report and that check.

149
00:09:22.410 --> 00:09:24.247
P F oh nine passes.

150
00:09:24.247 --> 00:09:28.369
P F ten fails: the hashes aren't
the ones in the promotion record,

151
00:09:28.369 --> 00:09:31.521
the smoke report failed
and ran in fixture mode,

152
00:09:31.521 --> 00:09:35.885
and promotion needs a live test
run; the evidence check failed too.

153
00:09:35.885 --> 00:09:40.735
A fixture report, or a stage with
unevidenced rows, can never promote.

154
00:09:40.735 --> 00:09:45.760
Step seven runs the tests, forty-six
pass, and step eight cleans up.

155
00:09:49.510 --> 00:09:52.198
Lab 2 is the live test stage itself.

156
00:09:52.198 --> 00:09:53.964
You need an Entra test tenant,

157
00:09:53.964 --> 00:09:57.499
an Azure test subscription
with Arc-enabled test servers,

158
00:09:57.499 --> 00:10:01.623
and a test Active Directory forest,
never production, plus the three

159
00:10:01.623 --> 00:10:06.484
identities and the objects the chapter
lists as made before the runbook starts.

160
00:10:06.484 --> 00:10:10.657
Load the test stage's settings,
sign in as the deploy identity,

161
00:10:10.657 --> 00:10:13.660
and run the pre-flight
check dry and then live.

162
00:10:13.660 --> 00:10:19.133
Then follow the twelve steps of Stage 1:
the baseline evidence, the HR middleware,

163
00:10:19.133 --> 00:10:24.274
dynamic rules, Lifecycle Workflows
created disabled, Group Policy as code,

164
00:10:24.274 --> 00:10:29.332
machine configuration in audit, Ansible
in check mode, the drift pipeline,

165
00:10:29.332 --> 00:10:34.722
the smoke test, the evidence package, the
rollback drill and the evidence check.

166
00:10:34.722 --> 00:10:39.923
To back it out, step eleven runs the rollback
written from the record, newest first.

167
00:10:39.923 --> 00:10:42.320
Nobody has run Lab 2 yet.

168
00:10:46.070 --> 00:10:47.457
The federal requirement.

169
00:10:47.457 --> 00:10:54.183
The controls are from NIST SP 800-53,
Revision 5, at the Moderate baseline.

170
00:10:54.183 --> 00:10:57.146
Every runbook step has
a requirements table,

171
00:10:57.146 --> 00:11:02.032
and every step leaves an evidence record
that names its controls and KSIs.

172
00:11:02.032 --> 00:11:06.995
The pre-flight check meets C M three,
enhancement two: the settings, kits,

173
00:11:06.995 --> 00:11:10.674
identities and cloud are
checked before any change,

174
00:11:10.674 --> 00:11:14.182
and pre-flight dot json is
the evidence, dry and live.

175
00:11:14.182 --> 00:11:18.973
Its rows P F oh two and P F oh five
show no secret in the settings file and

176
00:11:18.973 --> 00:11:24.771
three certificate identities, for
authenticator management and least privilege.

177
00:11:24.771 --> 00:11:29.352
The smoke test reads the gates' checks
again after the change and answers

178
00:11:29.352 --> 00:11:33.858
go or no-go, for the verification of
controls and continuous monitoring.

179
00:11:33.858 --> 00:11:39.182
One evidence package with a hash per file,
verified, protects the audit information.

180
00:11:39.182 --> 00:11:43.108
And every change is recorded
with its rollback, newest first.

181
00:11:43.108 --> 00:11:46.988
The KSI column is from FedRAMP's
Key Security Indicators,

182
00:11:46.988 --> 00:11:50.455
which are indicators for
cloud service providers;

183
00:11:50.455 --> 00:11:56.020
Chapter 10 uses only KSI IDs this workbook
already maps to the same controls.

184
00:11:59.770 --> 00:12:01.745
Check yourself with two questions.

185
00:12:01.745 --> 00:12:05.795
First: the smoke test runs on
fixtures with the fixture option.

186
00:12:05.795 --> 00:12:07.882
Can its report promote to production?

187
00:12:07.882 --> 00:12:09.719
The answer is b: no.

188
00:12:09.719 --> 00:12:13.380
Production's pre-flight check
needs a live, passing test run,

189
00:12:13.380 --> 00:12:16.658
and a fixture record proves
nothing about the stage.

190
00:12:16.658 --> 00:12:19.558
You saw P F ten refuse it in step six.

191
00:12:19.558 --> 00:12:23.357
Second: rollback add writes an
evidence record for a step.

192
00:12:23.357 --> 00:12:26.094
Why does the evidence check
still mark that row missing?

193
00:12:26.094 --> 00:12:28.511
The answer is a: the record is recorded,

194
00:12:28.511 --> 00:12:32.720
which proves the change was written
down, not that the step worked.

195
00:12:32.720 --> 00:12:36.620
That's why only two of seventeen
rows were evidenced in step five.

196
00:12:36.620 --> 00:12:40.888
The module page has two more questions,
on the three identities and on what

197
00:12:40.888 --> 00:12:46.000
production's pre-flight check compares,
each linked to the section that answers it.

198
00:12:49.750 --> 00:12:50.575
To sum up.

199
00:12:50.575 --> 00:12:53.873
You dry-ran the runbook's own
scripts: a pre-flight check,

200
00:12:53.873 --> 00:12:58.563
changes recorded with their rollback, a
smoke test that says no-go on fixtures,

201
00:12:58.563 --> 00:13:02.521
an evidence check against the
requirement rows, and a production

202
00:13:02.521 --> 00:13:06.625
pre-flight that refuses to promote
without a live, passing test stage.

203
00:13:06.625 --> 00:13:11.446
The evidence an ISSO keeps, for each
stage: the settings file and its hash,

204
00:13:11.446 --> 00:13:15.778
the pre-flight results, the
validation kit runs before and after,

205
00:13:15.778 --> 00:13:21.089
each kit's outputs, the smoke report, the
evidence package and its verify result,

206
00:13:21.089 --> 00:13:24.031
the deploy record and
the rollback scripts,

207
00:13:24.031 --> 00:13:27.300
and the evidence folder
with its evidence check.

208
00:13:27.300 --> 00:13:30.001
For production, add the
promotion record and

209
00:13:30.001 --> 00:13:32.775
seven days of chained
drift packages per wave.

210
00:13:32.775 --> 00:13:36.438
Before you run a stage, work
through the Chapter 10 checklist.

211
00:13:36.438 --> 00:13:38.587
And that's the end of the learning path.

212
00:13:38.587 --> 00:13:43.440
The path's index and the Labs page
list every module and every lab.
